1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
They're all around us and perform some of our toughest jobs.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:13,000
Whether they pack an industrial-sized punch or feature the finest precision,

3
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:18,000
they're an essential part of modern, everyday life.

4
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:23,000
And civilization couldn't have progressed without them.

5
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:26,000
They give us superhuman strength,

6
00:00:26,001 --> 00:00:29,000
help us push ourselves to the limit,

7
00:00:29,001 --> 00:00:32,420
And they do the work that can't be done by man alone.

8
00:00:34,140 --> 00:00:35,820
The link should be obvious.

9
00:00:36,340 --> 00:00:40,280
It's the untold story of Ropes and Chains on Modern Marvels.

10
00:00:51,740 --> 00:00:53,540
From scaling new heights

11
00:00:53,541 --> 00:00:57,140
to producing extra muscle when we need it most,

12
00:00:57,141 --> 00:01:02,140
We've always turned to ropes and chains.

13
00:01:02,141 --> 00:01:08,140
Whether it's simply for fun or to keep things under control,

14
00:01:08,141 --> 00:01:15,140
we use these symbols of strength, these not-so-distant cousins, in countless ways.

15
00:01:15,141 --> 00:01:18,140
Rope is among our earliest tools.

16
00:01:18,141 --> 00:01:22,140
It's even believed to outdate written history itself.

17
00:01:22,141 --> 00:01:33,440
Ancient civilizations discovered that by stringing together natural fibers, they could carry huge objects impossible to move by using human strength alone.

18
00:01:34,820 --> 00:01:39,540
If it wasn't for ropes, the new world may never have been discovered.

19
00:01:40,140 --> 00:01:46,960
For early explorers, ropes were invaluable to hoist the sails and make the voyages possible.

20
00:01:46,961 --> 00:01:52,960
Coils of rope were even used on Christopher Columbus' ships as makeshift beds,

21
00:01:52,961 --> 00:01:56,960
so sailors didn't have to sleep directly on a cold, wet deck.

22
00:01:58,960 --> 00:02:03,960
Throughout history, rope maintained its dominance as the workhorse of the seas.

23
00:02:04,960 --> 00:02:08,960
Still today, in ports around the world, rope is everywhere.

24
00:02:10,960 --> 00:02:15,960
And there's one supplier whose maritime connections span more than a century.

25
00:02:16,960 --> 00:02:23,960
Samson Rope in Ferndale, Washington has been braiding rope for a hundred and thirty years.

26
00:02:23,961 --> 00:02:27,960
The company holds the oldest trademark in the U.S.

27
00:02:27,961 --> 00:02:33,960
Samson makes rope for everything from fire and rescue missions to the logging industry.

28
00:02:33,961 --> 00:02:38,960
We probably have well over a thousand different products and different applications.

29
00:02:38,961 --> 00:02:44,960
But where they've really left their mark is making lines for the shipping industry.

30
00:02:44,961 --> 00:02:50,520
While deck crews have relied on rope for thousands of years, the use of modern

31
00:02:50,521 --> 00:02:57,800
synthetic rope is only about half a century old. Until the 1940s, ropes were

32
00:02:57,801 --> 00:03:03,080
made from natural fibers, including hemp and manila, derived from a plant grown in

33
00:03:03,081 --> 00:03:07,880
the Philippines. But when the US initially lost the Philippine Islands to Japan in

34
00:03:07,881 --> 00:03:14,680
World War II, manila supplies dropped and a substitute was needed. The DuPont

35
00:03:14,681 --> 00:03:19,800
company developed a nylon filament that was soon used in rope and parachute lines.

36
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:27,560
Another turning point in rope making came in 1957 when Samson, then called Samson Cordage Works,

37
00:03:27,960 --> 00:03:30,850
created the first synthetic double-braided rope.

38
00:03:30,851 --> 00:03:33,860
With a braided core inside an outer braided layer,

39
00:03:34,560 --> 00:03:36,460
double braiding provided added strength.

40
00:03:36,461 --> 00:03:44,960
Today, Samson produces both single and double-braided synthetic ropes.

41
00:03:47,420 --> 00:03:51,300
But no matter the style, the building blocks are the same.

42
00:03:53,700 --> 00:03:58,060
This is the first production step. It's called forming the first twist package.

43
00:03:59,180 --> 00:04:03,600
Multiple threads come together to make twisted strands that will later become rope.

44
00:04:03,601 --> 00:04:10,600
You can see inside the machine, the strand is multiplied in size.

45
00:04:10,601 --> 00:04:13,600
We've twisted several together to make a strand this size.

46
00:04:13,601 --> 00:04:17,600
With each additional strand, the strength is increased.

47
00:04:17,601 --> 00:04:21,600
The thread packages are then transferred to the winding station,

48
00:04:21,601 --> 00:04:24,600
where they're spun onto the braider bobbin.

49
00:04:24,601 --> 00:04:37,820
The real fun starts when the frenetic bobbins get going.

50
00:04:37,821 --> 00:04:43,080
The machine performs a complex dance with two dozen bobbins going left and right in a maypole

51
00:04:43,081 --> 00:04:44,080
formation.

52
00:04:44,081 --> 00:04:49,100
The braiding comes together at the top, and a rope is bored.

53
00:04:55,740 --> 00:05:01,500
Synthetic rope has become so durable that in some cases, it's even replacing wire cable.

54
00:05:02,120 --> 00:05:05,420
This is a 10-inch circumference M-Steel blue rope.

55
00:05:05,421 --> 00:05:10,660
It's a single braid, 12-strand, with a braid strength a little over a million pounds.

56
00:05:10,661 --> 00:05:17,940
Use it in the maritime industry with a tugboat or an ocean liner or any other marine uses.

57
00:05:18,560 --> 00:05:24,620
The rope, known as Amsteel Blue, contains the same tough fiber found in bulletproof vests.

58
00:05:25,680 --> 00:05:30,260
Some shipping companies who have switched from heavy wire cables to the much lighter rope

59
00:05:30,261 --> 00:05:34,780
report a drop in mooring times from three hours to 30 minutes.

60
00:05:34,781 --> 00:05:41,260
No matter where they're going to end up, before any Samson ropes head out to customers,

61
00:05:42,420 --> 00:05:46,200
they must be put to the test, or in other words, torn apart.

62
00:05:46,980 --> 00:05:51,780
A hydraulic tensile tester machine that can apply up to 1 million pounds of resistance

63
00:05:51,781 --> 00:05:53,700
determines the rope's strength.

64
00:05:54,160 --> 00:05:59,800
We want to know how strong the rope is because the working load is based on the actual strength.

65
00:05:59,801 --> 00:06:05,140
The test rope is pulled ten times to align the fibers for a more reliable test.

66
00:06:05,940 --> 00:06:07,960
Okay, it's going up to break now.

67
00:06:09,120 --> 00:06:13,300
The test ends with a bang once the rope has reached its breaking point.

68
00:06:17,320 --> 00:06:23,740
The rope passed the test, exceeding its declared resistance strength before any fibers tore.

69
00:06:24,280 --> 00:06:28,120
You can feel the heat that came from the energy when it was broken.

70
00:06:28,920 --> 00:06:29,700
So it did good.

71
00:06:29,800 --> 00:06:36,800
Once Samson's ropes meet their tough standards, they're prepped for shipment.

72
00:06:36,801 --> 00:06:42,800
19,000 miles of Samson rope are shipped out every year.

73
00:06:42,801 --> 00:06:49,800
You can find their ropes in every major port in the world, like the Port of Baltimore,

74
00:06:49,801 --> 00:06:56,800
where tugboats use the lines to move ships hundreds, even thousands of times their size.

75
00:06:56,801 --> 00:06:58,660
This is the tug line that we use.

76
00:06:59,240 --> 00:07:01,100
Basically, it's made by Samson.

77
00:07:01,320 --> 00:07:02,480
It's a synthetic line.

78
00:07:02,640 --> 00:07:05,560
It will roughly pull 508,000 pounds.

79
00:07:05,880 --> 00:07:07,780
This thing is two years old right now.

80
00:07:07,860 --> 00:07:09,320
They should last four to five years.

81
00:07:10,040 --> 00:07:12,840
This is really what is the strength of our tugboat here.

82
00:07:14,100 --> 00:07:17,040
The tugboats need up to 100 feet of rope

83
00:07:17,041 --> 00:07:19,400
to stretch to the ships they guide into port.

84
00:07:20,720 --> 00:07:22,520
Once ship and tug are connected,

85
00:07:23,100 --> 00:07:25,040
it's time to claim a space on the pier.

86
00:07:25,041 --> 00:07:29,540
using ropes to close the final 10 to 15 foot gap.

87
00:07:30,060 --> 00:07:32,600
They pass this to us, and then from the ship,

88
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:36,680
it is attached to this line here, which is called a pennant.

89
00:07:37,540 --> 00:07:40,400
And then they'll keep the line up and make it secure to the pier.

90
00:07:41,680 --> 00:07:43,980
Newer synthetic ropes float in water,

91
00:07:44,440 --> 00:07:48,520
a big plus for crews thanks to their lighter weight and greater safety.

92
00:07:49,060 --> 00:07:52,740
The lines important to these floating is that they would get into the propulsion gear

93
00:07:52,741 --> 00:07:55,680
and could stop the vessels or cause catastrophic event on the deck.

94
00:07:56,540 --> 00:07:59,240
Every day, the rope lines here prove their worth.

95
00:07:59,980 --> 00:08:03,160
And a port without ropes would be a pretty lonely place.

96
00:08:04,020 --> 00:08:06,720
Well, the importance of lines is if the ship doesn't have lines,

97
00:08:06,780 --> 00:08:08,500
it'll be floating away in the water with a tide.

98
00:08:15,200 --> 00:08:18,960
King mail was used in battle for over 2,000 years

99
00:08:18,961 --> 00:08:21,020
before peaking in the Middle Ages.

100
00:08:21,021 --> 00:08:24,040
The thousands of tiny links were each made by hand,

101
00:08:24,440 --> 00:08:27,500
and a complete suit could weigh up to 50 pounds.

102
00:08:28,060 --> 00:08:31,020
Ropes and Chains will continue on Modern Marvels.

103
00:08:31,300 --> 00:08:35,280
We now return to Ropes and Chains on Modern Marvels.

104
00:08:37,320 --> 00:08:42,800
The untold stories of ropes and chains have a common bond in hard labor.

105
00:08:44,200 --> 00:08:48,660
When it comes to heavy lifting, ropes have always been tied to tough work.

106
00:08:51,020 --> 00:08:57,260
But when a really difficult job comes along, sometimes there's no choice but to go heavy metal.

107
00:09:01,120 --> 00:09:06,240
Visit any port or canal in the world, and you'll see chain at nearly every turn.

108
00:09:11,740 --> 00:09:20,080
Just ask the dock crews at the port of Baltimore, and they'll tell you that for many jobs, it's no chain, no game.

109
00:09:21,020 --> 00:09:28,920
Each chain has a hook on one end and a hook on the other end.

110
00:09:29,100 --> 00:09:30,240
They're good for five ton.

111
00:09:31,020 --> 00:09:34,660
So if you take the total weight of the cargo, it's 50 ton.

112
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:36,160
They're crisscrossed.

113
00:09:36,520 --> 00:09:39,040
That gives you lateral and longitudinal pull.

114
00:09:40,040 --> 00:09:41,180
So the cargo is secured.

115
00:09:42,060 --> 00:09:45,240
Once on the ships, chains play an equally critical role.

116
00:09:46,300 --> 00:09:48,240
Onboard the Norwegian ship Tamerlane,

117
00:09:48,241 --> 00:09:57,300
chains hold one very invaluable and heavy piece of gear in place anchor it

118
00:09:57,301 --> 00:10:05,280
weights about 9.3 tons our ship weighs about 23,000 ton and this anchor can

119
00:10:05,281 --> 00:10:08,000
hold the whole ship

120
00:10:13,200 --> 00:10:18,180
the massive metal chain is constantly in touch with the elements but it's tough

121
00:10:18,181 --> 00:10:25,620
means maintenance is rare there's a lot of rust but every five year the ship goes into dry dock

122
00:10:25,621 --> 00:10:34,620
and then we have to paint it as you can see there's a lot of rust right now making industrial

123
00:10:34,621 --> 00:10:43,320
chains that can stand up to anything is no easy business at peerless chain in Winona Minnesota

124
00:10:43,321 --> 00:10:47,740
just looking at their 350,000-square-foot production floor

125
00:10:47,741 --> 00:10:50,460
tells you this is heavy-duty work.

126
00:10:54,760 --> 00:10:57,740
Here, unassuming thin metal coil

127
00:10:57,741 --> 00:11:00,580
is transformed into tough, rugged chain links

128
00:11:00,581 --> 00:11:02,160
for all kinds of uses.

129
00:11:04,260 --> 00:11:07,460
We make chain for a variety of different applications,

130
00:11:07,720 --> 00:11:11,020
everything from securing fighters on the deck of an aircraft carrier

131
00:11:11,021 --> 00:11:15,020
to securing Bradley fighting vehicles in a C-130 transport

132
00:11:15,021 --> 00:11:19,060
to swing set chain to tire chains for passenger cars.

133
00:11:20,700 --> 00:11:23,620
Peerless Chain has been making links since 1917.

134
00:11:24,620 --> 00:11:29,260
Early business consisted of making tire chains by hand in a one-room office.

135
00:11:31,220 --> 00:11:34,300
Today, they're the largest industrial chainmaker in North America.

136
00:11:36,040 --> 00:11:38,040
Every day that we're running here at Peerless,

137
00:11:38,041 --> 00:11:43,040
we consume approximately 80 tons of steel or rod.

138
00:11:43,080 --> 00:11:46,720
That translates into about 40 miles of chain every day.

139
00:11:46,740 --> 00:11:50,280
That equates to about 2.9 million welds.

140
00:11:56,120 --> 00:11:59,020
Peerless chain carries on a craft that in the U.S.,

141
00:11:59,060 --> 00:12:02,560
only blacksmiths once performed.

142
00:12:02,600 --> 00:12:04,740
But chain's long history can be traced back

143
00:12:04,760 --> 00:12:07,160
to references in the Bible.

144
00:12:07,161 --> 00:12:11,760
It's believed chains were used to draw water from wells over 2,000 years ago.

145
00:12:12,880 --> 00:12:17,960
And Leonardo da Vinci's 16th century notebooks include early sketches of roller chain,

146
00:12:18,520 --> 00:12:20,400
similar to what bicycles use today.

147
00:12:21,560 --> 00:12:25,500
Chains were such a symbol of strength that in the early 1900s,

148
00:12:25,840 --> 00:12:29,420
magician Harry Houdini made an entire career out of breaking out of them.

149
00:12:30,820 --> 00:12:34,920
And chains spurred industrial progress and helped propel the engine

150
00:12:34,921 --> 00:12:37,520
that got the Wright brothers off the ground in 1903.

151
00:12:41,100 --> 00:12:44,240
More infamously, chains have been used for punishment,

152
00:12:45,140 --> 00:12:48,380
and throughout history, they've kept prisoners from escaping.

153
00:12:51,440 --> 00:12:55,160
A peerless chain, churning out as many links as they do

154
00:12:55,161 --> 00:12:59,000
means feeding the plant's insatiable appetite for raw steel.

155
00:12:59,001 --> 00:13:01,380
This is where all of our raw material comes in.

156
00:13:01,381 --> 00:13:07,800
And each one of these coils, and it doesn't matter what size the material is, weigh approximately 4,000 pounds.

157
00:13:08,480 --> 00:13:12,600
And in any given day of production, we will go through 40 coils.

158
00:13:13,320 --> 00:13:17,060
The raw coils arrive from the steel mills with some scale on their surface,

159
00:13:17,340 --> 00:13:21,420
so they must be cleaned and prepped before their transformation begins.

160
00:13:25,020 --> 00:13:30,620
A 20-minute soak in 5,000 gallons of sulfuric acid usually does the trick.

161
00:13:31,380 --> 00:13:41,460
Once the acid is washed away, the coil is dunked again in a lime bath to smooth the surface.

162
00:13:43,060 --> 00:13:47,680
The supersized coil is then whisked away to the wire draw department.

163
00:13:51,560 --> 00:13:57,280
There, the coils, resembling giant slinkies, are ready to be cut down to size.

164
00:13:57,281 --> 00:14:04,860
We're taking those coils that we cleaned and prepped, and we're drawing the wire down to a very specific diameter.

165
00:14:05,980 --> 00:14:11,740
The huge rod is uncoiled and fed into a machine that narrows it down with a smooth, shiny finish.

166
00:14:13,440 --> 00:14:15,800
Then, it's time to make some chains.

167
00:14:19,640 --> 00:14:25,340
Right now, we're in the forming and welding department, which is really the heart and soul of the chain-making process.

168
00:14:25,341 --> 00:14:32,740
This is where we take that rod that we prepped, cleaned, and drawn, and we form it and weld it into chains.

169
00:14:33,580 --> 00:14:39,580
Here, the wire is cut into slugs that are squeezed shut into the right diameter for the customer's needs.

170
00:14:43,560 --> 00:14:47,220
The links then get a blast of heat to strengthen the enclosure.

171
00:14:47,900 --> 00:14:53,320
It's formed into a chain, then it goes to the next piece of equipment, which is a welder,

172
00:14:53,321 --> 00:14:56,160
where it's welded with the electric resistance method.

173
00:14:56,161 --> 00:15:00,220
It's heated up to about 1,800 degrees, welded in trim.

174
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:07,460
Peerless makes chains that need to work in tough environments.

175
00:15:08,360 --> 00:15:13,100
So the links get added protection in the fiery galvanizing process.

176
00:15:13,960 --> 00:15:16,920
We heat the sink to 850 degrees.

177
00:15:17,400 --> 00:15:19,480
The chain is run through the molten sink.

178
00:15:19,481 --> 00:15:25,320
It puts a protective coating that inhibits rusting in tough environments like salt water.

179
00:15:26,480 --> 00:15:30,480
The zinc plating adheres as the links run through the flames,

180
00:15:30,800 --> 00:15:33,960
producing the tough skin chain that Peerless is known for.

181
00:15:35,380 --> 00:15:40,860
The chains must then face the flames once again when they're heat treated for further strengthening.

182
00:15:41,960 --> 00:15:47,000
Taking the chain, we're heating it to about 17 to 1800 degrees,

183
00:15:47,001 --> 00:15:53,540
quenching it in water, reheat treating in a temper of about 500 to 600 degrees,

184
00:15:53,541 --> 00:15:55,100
and then cooling it again.

185
00:15:55,101 --> 00:15:59,760
We do this to give the chain extra hardness, durability, and ductility.

186
00:15:59,761 --> 00:16:04,640
But the chain can't head to customers until it's proven its strength.

187
00:16:04,641 --> 00:16:10,760
All of our welded chain is pulled or proof tested to twice the working load limit.

188
00:16:10,761 --> 00:16:15,440
We ensure the integrity of the chain by proof testing every single link

189
00:16:15,441 --> 00:16:17,000
before it goes out the door.

190
00:16:17,980 --> 00:16:22,580
For Peerless, the proof test is one of their most critical steps.

191
00:16:23,300 --> 00:16:26,440
I'm proof testing these chains that we're shipping out to our troops overseas.

192
00:16:26,860 --> 00:16:29,300
They have to hold 10,200 pounds.

193
00:16:30,260 --> 00:16:35,120
Every chain is tested and must survive well beyond its declared strength limit.

194
00:16:35,620 --> 00:16:40,320
If we're making a chain that we tell you has a working load limit of 4,000 pounds,

195
00:16:40,321 --> 00:16:44,820
we will proof test and pull that chain to twice the working load limit.

196
00:16:45,440 --> 00:16:52,440
In other words, we will pull that chain, every link of that to 8,000 pounds.

197
00:16:52,441 --> 00:16:57,440
The chain making process has stood the test of time.

198
00:16:57,441 --> 00:17:01,440
It's kind of like the thing, if it's not broke, you know, don't fix it.

199
00:17:01,441 --> 00:17:03,440
And that's the way chain making is.

200
00:17:03,441 --> 00:17:08,440
It's worked for a long time and continues to today to make a very safe, durable product.

201
00:17:15,440 --> 00:17:21,406
Chain gangs began in Georgia in 1908, but fell out of favor around the U.S.

202
00:17:21,407 --> 00:17:22,440
by the 1950s.

203
00:17:23,100 --> 00:17:28,040
Alabama reintroduced them in 1995, before a legal challenge ended the practice.

204
00:17:28,900 --> 00:17:31,520
Ropes and Chains will continue on Modern Marvels.

205
00:17:32,320 --> 00:17:36,280
We now return to Ropes and Chains on Modern Marvels.

206
00:17:38,160 --> 00:17:42,900
Ropes have always been associated with the biggest jobs and toughest tasks.

207
00:17:42,901 --> 00:17:48,000
And there are few tasks tougher than cleaning the world's largest structures.

208
00:17:49,900 --> 00:17:56,480
One German company has taken on this colossal task, free of charge, for over 30 years.

209
00:17:57,240 --> 00:18:03,460
Kertcher has donated its expertise in high-pressure cleaning at over 80 historical monuments around the globe.

210
00:18:07,760 --> 00:18:09,180
From Rio de Janeiro,

211
00:18:09,181 --> 00:18:12,820
to Rome's St. Peter's Square,

212
00:18:12,900 --> 00:18:18,140
to ancient statues in Luxor, Egypt.

213
00:18:20,060 --> 00:18:22,460
But the first ever cleaning of Mount Rushmore

214
00:18:22,461 --> 00:18:24,160
was perhaps their proudest,

215
00:18:24,580 --> 00:18:27,300
and this daring work couldn't be done without a lot of rope

216
00:18:27,301 --> 00:18:29,940
and healthy respect for the dangers involved.

217
00:18:32,960 --> 00:18:35,120
No one can tell me they have no fear,

218
00:18:35,480 --> 00:18:37,020
but it's part of the job.

219
00:18:37,760 --> 00:18:38,720
It makes you safer.

220
00:18:38,721 --> 00:18:49,080
In 2005, a half mile of rope aided 10 German specialists from Kertcher as they cleaned the 22,000 square foot surface.

221
00:18:51,320 --> 00:19:00,320
Using just pressurized water and no chemicals, the workers removed algae and other organic stains while dangling 500 feet off the ground.

222
00:19:03,560 --> 00:19:08,080
They completed the massive presidential facelift in just five weeks.

223
00:19:08,720 --> 00:19:14,540
If you're doing one of these high-flying jobs, you want to make sure you're safe.

224
00:19:15,340 --> 00:19:19,580
And that's where Gravitech Systems, outside of Seattle, Washington, can help.

225
00:19:25,180 --> 00:19:31,360
Gravitech has been training people in high-altitude and high-profile jobs for over 20 years.

226
00:19:32,120 --> 00:19:35,360
We've conducted training for people at NASA Kennedy Space Center

227
00:19:35,361 --> 00:19:38,300
on the use of vertical lifelines for the tops of their buildings.

